{% extends "base.peb" %}
{% block title %}Authorized Vendors{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
    <h1>Women, Infants and Children (WIC) Authorized Vendors</h1>
    <p class="lead">Source: <a
            href="https://data.chhs.ca.gov/dataset/women-infants-and-children-wic-authorized-vendors">data.chhs.ca.gov/dataset/women-infants-and-children-wic-authorized-vendors</a>
    </p>

    <h2>About the dataset</h2>
    <p>This dataset contains the vendor name and location of all currently active vendors authorized to offer
        supplemental foods to participants for redemption with WIC food instruments and vouchers.</p>
    <p>The Women, Infants and Children (WIC) Program is a federally-funded health and nutrition program that provides
        assistance to pregnant women, new mothers, infants and children under age five. WIC helps California families by
        providing food instruments and vouchers that can be used to purchase healthy supplemental foods from over 4000
        WIC-authorized vendor stores throughout the State. WIC also provides nutritional education, breastfeeding
        support and help finding healthcare and other community services. Participants must meet income guidelines and
        other criteria. Currently, 84 WIC agencies provide services monthly to over 1.45 million participants at over
        650 sites in local communities throughout the State.</p>

    <h2>Published files</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>File</th>
            <th>Rows</th>
            <th>Contents</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>women-infants-and-children-wic-authorized-vendors-.csv</code></td>
            <td>3,687</td>
            <td>Active vendors with their address and location.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>women-infants-and-children-wic-authorized-vendors--data-dictionary.csv</code></td>
            <td></td>
            <td>Column descriptions.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>datapackage.json</code></td>
            <td></td>
            <td>Dataset metadata.</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How it is stored in our database</h2>
    <p>Loaded by <code>db/changelog/seed/01-vendors.yaml</code> into the <code>vendor</code> table, which drives the
        WICit map.</p>
    <h3><code>vendor</code></h3>
    <p>3,687 rows. Indexed on <code>(latitude, longitude)</code> for map viewport queries, and on <code>county</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Column</th>
            <th>Type</th>
            <th>Published column</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>id</code></td>
            <td>bigint</td>
            <td>Generated key. The dataset has no vendor ID.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>name</code></td>
            <td>varchar(200)</td>
            <td>VENDOR, with its quotes removed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>address</code></td>
            <td>varchar(200)</td>
            <td>ADDRESS</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>second_address</code></td>
            <td>varchar(200)</td>
            <td>SECOND ADDRESS. Empty in the current file, so always <code>NULL</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>city</code></td>
            <td>varchar(100)</td>
            <td>CITY</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>zip</code></td>
            <td>varchar(10)</td>
            <td>ZIP. Text, to keep leading zeros. 19 vendors have none.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>county</code></td>
            <td>varchar(50)</td>
            <td>COUNTY</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>latitude</code></td>
            <td>double precision</td>
            <td>LATITUDE. 42 vendors have no location and are not shown on the map.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>longitude</code></td>
            <td>double precision</td>
            <td>LONGITUDE</td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Why this file is loaded differently</h3>
    <p>Liquibase's CSV parser rejects this file, for three reasons:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Every value is padded with spaces to a fixed width, including after the closing quote of a quoted name:
            <code>"SAVE MART #609"&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;,11980 HIGHWAY 88&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;,</code></li>
        <li>It is encoded as Windows-1252, not UTF-8. For example, the apostrophe in <code>RAY’S FOOD PLACE</code> is
            the single byte <code>0x92</code>.
        </li>
        <li>It ends with a blank line and a <code>3687 rows selected.</code> footer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So the changeset reads the file as Windows-1252 and loads each line whole into a one-column staging table. In the
        database, runs of spaces are collapsed, then <code>wic_csv_fields()</code> splits each line, accepting the
        padding around quoted values. Only lines with exactly eight fields and a name and address become vendors, which
        drops the footer. Three vendor names contain a comma, such as <code>NORTH COAST COOPERATIVE, INC</code>, and are
        split correctly because the comma is inside quotes.</p>

    <h2>API</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Request</th>
            <th>Returns</th>
        </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GET /api/vendors/in-bounds?west=&amp;south=&amp;east=&amp;north=</code></td>
            <td>Vendors inside a map viewport, ordered by name. Takes <code>limit</code> (default 500, at most 1,000).
                The map calls this each time it moves.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>GET /api/vendors</code></td>
            <td>Pages of vendors ordered by name. Takes <code>county</code>, <code>city</code>, <code>page</code> and
                <code>size</code>.
            </td>
        </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <pre><code>GET /api/vendors/in-bounds?west=-121.6&amp;south=38.4&amp;east=-121.3&amp;north=38.7</code></pre>
    <pre><code>SELECT name, address, city, zip, latitude, longitude
FROM vendor
WHERE latitude BETWEEN 38.4 AND 38.7
  AND longitude BETWEEN -121.6 AND -121.3
ORDER BY name
LIMIT 500;</code></pre>
{% endblock %}
